    function timingRules(){return '<details><summary class="cursor-interaction">How timing changes the next round</summary><p>Each question records time until your first valid answer and total active time. Breaks and hidden-tab time are excluded. Visible-screen distractions cannot be detected; mark an interrupted question to exclude its timing.</p><p>The six-question calibration starts at level 4 and probes up after an independently correct first answer, or down after an error or support. Skips alone do not lower a level. It provides a provisional practice starting point; you can change it in Settings. Calibration is excluded from practice timing baselines.</p><p>Addition and subtraction adapt separately during practice. We compare the same level and calculation format. Eight recent answers across at least two rounds need at least seven first answers correct and six independently correct before a step up.</p><p>With eight independently correct timed answers, we compare the latest four with the median of earlier answers at that level. If three take over 1.5 times your earlier median, the next round mixes familiar and harder questions. Two independently correct harder answers then establish the new level, regardless of speed.</p><p>No countdown, speed points or automatic level reduction for slow answers. These are adjustable practice rules, not a validated assessment of ability or attention.</p></details>';}
